# `std::alloc` -- a thin, deliberately *non-generic* wrapper around
# `core::platform::GlobalAllocator`, used by every generic collection in this
# package instead of calling the gap directly from their own generic
# methods.
#
# This indirection was originally added as a workaround for a real
# codegen bug: any generic instantiation whose *template* lived in an
# extern package (every `std` type, from a consuming application's point
# of view) was silently dropped from codegen entirely, panicking the
# moment anything referenced it. That bug is now fixed at the compiler
# level (`Driver::compile`'s generic-instantiation merge loop, see
# `docs/10-modules-and-linkage.md`'s "Fixed: two bugs found building
# `std`..." section) -- calling `GlobalAllocator::*` directly from a
# generic method no longer panics.
#
# The indirection is kept anyway, on separately confirmed grounds: `std`
# is compiled as its own whole-package translation unit ahead of any one
# consumer (`std.o`), so `String`'s own internal use of `List<u8>`, for
# instance, is compiled into `std.o` regardless of whether a given
# consumer ever touches `String`. Empirically, removing this layer and
# calling `GlobalAllocator::*` straight from each generic method made gap
# reachability *strictly worse* -- more distinct symbol references to the
# gap baked into `std.o` itself, not fewer -- so it stays, now purely as
# an ordinary abstraction boundary rather than a bug workaround.

internal alloc(size: usize) => *mut u8 {
    GlobalAllocator::alloc(size)
}

internal free(ptr: *u8) => void {
    GlobalAllocator::free(ptr);
}

internal realloc(ptr: *u8, size: usize) => *mut u8 {
    GlobalAllocator::realloc(ptr, size)
}
